      "math_academy_id": "e-3573",
      "problem": "What is the period of $y=\\cos\\left(\\dfrac x 2\\right)?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the function $y=\\cos\\left(\\dfrac x 2\\right)$ can be written as $y=\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} x\\right).$ So, it takes the form $y=\\cos(Bx)$ with $B = \\dfrac{1}{2}.$ \n\nThe horizontal scaling factor is given by \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{B} = \\dfrac{1}{\\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) } = 2.\n$$\n\nSo, the period is\n\n$$\n2\\pi \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{B} = 2\\pi \\cdot 2= 4\\pi.\n$$\n\nWe can verify this in the graph shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254788/assets/2696d7165277c7c0adf9e61fba0d4ac94e14d8290c7617b6fc268f801fe0431a.png)",
